1	/**
2	 * update-plan.ts — what taking a newer release onto this tree means, path by path.
3	 *
4	 * # The idea, and why nothing else had to be invented
5	 *
6	 * `.github/atomaton-release.json` records the release this tree came from. That
7	 * fact is all a mechanical update needs, because it makes the ordinary git
8	 * three-way merge available:
9	 *
10	 *     base    = the release the tree came from (its asset's files)
11	 *     ours    = the adopter's tree, as it stands
12	 *     theirs  = the release being taken (its asset's files)
13	 *
14	 * Everything an upgrade policy would otherwise hand-write falls out of the
15	 * comparison. A file the adopter never touched takes the new version silently. A
16	 * file the adopter changed and upstream did not keeps the adopter's words. A file
17	 * both sides changed needs a look — and that is the whole, exact definition of
18	 * "this update needs a person": not an upstream declaration about some average
19	 * adopter, but the collision this particular tree actually has. Upstream cannot
20	 * know whether an adopter customised `config.yaml`; only the tree can answer, and
21	 * only at update time.
22	 *
23	 * # What this module decides, and what it refuses to decide
24	 *
25	 * This is the classification layer: which paths changed upstream, which the
26	 * adopter touched, and which strategy each one takes. It does not perform merges
27	 * — "merge" entries are paths where the outcome is only knowable by running the
28	 * merge (`git merge-file`, in the machinery that applies this plan). It also never
29	 * proposes touching a path that is in neither release: the adopter's own files are
30	 * outside this computation entirely, which is why the plan needs no ownership
31	 * table and no namespace rules. A layout change upstream needs no change here.
32	 *
33	 * Pure: three content maps in, one plan out.
34	 */
